Z||000041||terryk||07-17-2000||07:05 PM||||The VSS generator goes to the speedo and them to the ECM on the Fiero so if the VSS now goes to the ECM, the speedo needs to get fed. It's very possible it will read way low (not enough pulses).<P>The 4T60E that came with the DOHC might be a 16k or 32k pulse output (30 tooth, something like that).<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000042||lowCG||07-17-2000||10:08 PM||||Terry, you're saying that by splitting the signal from the VSS,between the ECM and the speedo,that there may be a low reading?<BR>I managed to swap the wires around and got the speedo to at least work again,need to flush the tranny now as some grit fell in there when removing the VSS <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">,wishing now that I hadn't,and just ran the car on jackstands to try the VSS,oh well.<BR>The distance from the strut housing to the timing belt cover is about 11/16";plenty of room!<BR>I found a diagnostic plug on some poor car that won't be needing it anymore,but I had to remove the battery and get at the wires to install it on my car.By the time I got it all soldered up,it was too late to fire it up.I'll have to wait to drive it tomorrow morning and then see what codes I get.||||reg||1
Z||000043||terryk||07-17-2000||11:31 PM||||What I mean is that the number of pulse per mile from the VSS might be too low from the VSS. The Fiero uses 4000 (the cruise uses 2000).<P>The DOHC ECM expects the VSS to drive it and then it outputs a signal to the speedo. The Fiero is backwards, the Fiero speedo expects the VSS to be connected to it and it sends a signal to the ECM.<P>The VSS should be able to drive both the ECM and speedo. Your speedo will then be correct but the ECM may see the speed as being too low. Since I haven't played with the 96 and up stuff much, I can't be sure. Just trying to warn you.<P>To be frank, I believe you will be need to run an earlier ECM at some point it you want it to run right. The 91-93 ECM (16149396) supports manual trans and can be re-cal'd for the Fiero VSS. You don't use the mass, front crank sensor or cam sensor. You add a MAP. The injectors will not be run SFI, but rather batch. Not a big deal since the ECM switched to batch above about 3000 RPM anyway.<P>11/16"? Heck, that's a mile!! Until I get on it.<P> So you cut out the dogbone mount because it didn't align with anything?<P>I plan on a dog bone, I haven't gotten that far. LowCG beat me the b%st^rd!!!! He cheated!!!<P>Dan, I agree with the TB/clean comment. My SC idled like trash until I cleaned the TB. I now clean it every other month. I just love EGR!<P>Terry<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000044||lowCG||07-18-2000||12:34 AM||||I noticed that the intake manifold was pretty crusty on the inside,could be some soot/varnish in somewhere,I'll clean it tomorrow when I flush the gearbox.<BR>Terry,that's clearance to the front of the motor,so you shouldn't have any problems.<BR>I almost went with solid motor(trans)mounts,and let the urethane cradle bushings take up the slack,the stock mounts could easily be welded onto to do this.<BR>It also wouldn't take much to make a jig with wood to make your own mounts using some control arm bushings.I didn't see any practical ways to put the dogbone back in,not that it couldn't be done,just that I'd want to be sure that it was needed before the effort.||||reg||1
Z||000045||terryk||07-18-2000||01:01 AM||||Thanks LowCG (if that's your real name...),<P>I don't really want to hack out the stock Fiero dogbone mount but at the same time, it will take a real Rube Goldberg to make one work in that location.<P>I don't see any way the lower mounts can control the engine and I'm not thrilled about mounting one on the passenger compartment rear wall. There has to be a way to fabricate a cantilever below the stock DOHC location.<P>Ten bucks I just hack something in.....||||reg||14
Z||000046||dman||07-18-2000||04:07 PM||||Hello everyone. As a suggestion, have either of you spoken to Chris West? I've been to his shop and have talked to him a few times. He's a great guy and he's got great people working with/for him. He and his people talk straight and give you their opinions, no salesman's pitch or other crap. His own personal car is a 3.4 TDC(DOHC). He has a dogbone mount towards the drivers side (not the passenger side like on a 2.8 fiero). His 3.4 TDC fiero is clean, very very clean. <P> Oh, BTW, you guys are having way too much fun. These engine conversions you are describing are too cool! Continued good luck and success on your projects, and thanks for sharing your information here on the forum!<P>||||reg||7
